<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pachoras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Faras, ancient Pachoras, was a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, near the modern border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, now submerged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>. It was the capital of the kingdom of Nobadia and later the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> under Makuria. Polish archaeologists led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazimierz-michalowski">Kazimierz Michałowski</a> uncovered its cathedral in the 1960s, rescuing over a hundred wall paintings of the 8th to 14th centuries, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faras.jpg" alt="Faras" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Christian Gau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faras,_Franz_Christian_Gau.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
